1. Why do we need Advanced Locators?

Suppose a page has:

<button>Add to Cart</button>
<button>Add to Cart</button>
<button>Add to Cart</button>

Now this will fail:

await page.getByRole('button', {
  name: 'Add to Cart'
}).click();

Why?
Because Playwright found 3 matching elements.
It doesn't know which one you want.
This is called Strict Mode Violation.


2. first()
Select the first matching element.

await page
  .getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to Cart' })
  .first()
  .click();

Meaning:
[Button1] [Button2] [Button3]
    ↑
  Click this


3. last()
Select the last matching element.

await page
  .getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to Cart' })
  .last()
  .click();

Meaning:
[Button1] [Button2] [Button3]
                         ↑
                     Click this



                     
4. nth()
Select a specific index.

await page
  .getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to Cart' })
  .nth(1)
  .click();

Indexes start from 0.

0 → First
1 → Second
2 → Third

So:
.nth(1)
means second element.


6. Locator Chaining ⭐
HTML:
<div class="login-form">
    <input id="username">
</div>

Instead of:
page.locator('#username')

Use:
page
  .locator('.login-form')
  .locator('#username');

Meaning:
Find login form
      ↓
Find username inside it

This makes locators more specific.



7. filter() ⭐ Very Important

Suppose:
<div>
  <h3>iPhone</h3>
  <button>Add to Cart</button>
</div>

<div>
  <h3>Samsung</h3>
  <button>Add to Cart</button>
</div>
ou want the Samsung button.

await page
  .locator('div')
  .filter({ hasText: 'Samsung' })
  .getByRole('button', {
      name: 'Add to Cart'
  })
  .click();

Meaning:

Find Samsung card
      ↓
Inside it click Add to Cart


8. count()
Count matching elements.

const count =
  await page
    .getByRole('button')
    .count();

console.log(count);

Suppose there are 10 buttons.

Output:
10
